/* Bassiet book, Depth 16 part A: Firing Cells, Long Roads, Dark Territories. Original text. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c16-cells']={depth:16,title:'The Firing Cells',
epi:'Lightning jumps the black gaps faster than anything else in the body, because it is thought. Somewhere in the storm is the thing the Pall came for.',
c:['excitability','meningitis','multiple-sclerosis','catecholamines'],
S:[
{h:'Neurons and glia',fig:'neuroap',p:[
'A neuron\'s action potential begins when depolarisation at the axon hillock reaches threshold: voltage-gated **Na⁺ channels** open (rapid upstroke), then inactivate within a millisecond while **K⁺ channels** open (repolarisation, with an after-hyperpolarisation). During the **absolute refractory period** the Na⁺ channels are inactivated and no stimulus can fire the cell, which keeps the impulse travelling one way; in the relative refractory period a stronger stimulus can. At the terminal, voltage-gated Ca²⁺ entry triggers vesicle fusion through SNARE proteins (the target of botulinum toxin).',
'**Myelin** raises conduction speed by **saltatory conduction**: the impulse jumps between nodes of Ranvier, where Na⁺ channels cluster. **Oligodendrocytes** myelinate many CNS axons each (lost in multiple sclerosis, PML and the leukodystrophies; "fried egg" cells in oligodendroglioma). **Schwann cells** myelinate one segment of one peripheral axon (lost in Guillain-Barré syndrome; S-100 positive schwannomas) and guide regeneration. **Astrocytes** (GFAP positive) hold the blood-brain barrier, buffer K⁺, recycle glutamate, store glycogen and form the glial scar. **Microglia** are resident macrophages derived from the yolk sac; HIV infects them and forms multinucleated giant cells. **Ependymal** cells line the ventricles, and the **choroid plexus** makes CSF.',
'Injury. Cut an axon and the distal segment undergoes **Wallerian degeneration** (axon and myelin break down and are cleared); the cell body swells and its Nissl substance disperses (**central chromatolysis**) as it shifts to making repair proteins. Peripheral axons regrow at about 1–3 mm a day along their Schwann cell tubes; CNS axons do not, because the glial scar and myelin inhibitors block them. Neurons are permanent cells, and the ones most sensitive to hypoxia are hippocampal CA1 pyramidal cells, cerebellar Purkinje cells and neocortical layers 3, 5 and 6.'],
table:{cols:['Transmitter','Main source','Change in disease'],rows:[
['Acetylcholine','basal nucleus of Meynert, pontine nuclei, motor neurons','↓ Alzheimer, Huntington; relatively ↑ in Parkinson (hence antimuscarinics for tremor); ↑ in REM sleep'],
['Dopamine','substantia nigra pars compacta, ventral tegmental area, arcuate nucleus','↓ Parkinson; ↑ schizophrenia, Huntington; inhibits prolactin'],
['Noradrenaline','locus coeruleus (pons)','↑ anxiety, opioid withdrawal; ↓ depression'],
['Serotonin','raphe nuclei (brainstem midline)','↓ anxiety, depression'],
['Histamine','tuberomammillary nucleus (hypothalamus)','wakefulness; sedating antihistamines block it'],
['GABA','inhibitory interneurons everywhere; striatal neurons, cerebellar Purkinje cells','↓ anxiety, Huntington (striatum), seizures'],
['Glycine','spinal cord and brainstem interneurons','strychnine blocks its receptor; tetanus toxin blocks its release (spasm)'],
['Glutamate','most excitatory neurons','excess → excitotoxicity in stroke and ALS; NMDA receptors (memantine, ketamine block them)']]},
s:[['bio','Building the messengers: tyrosine → L-DOPA (tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting step) → dopamine (DOPA decarboxylase, vitamin B6) → noradrenaline (dopamine β-hydroxylase, vitamin C) → adrenaline (PNMT, induced by cortisol in the adrenal medulla). Tryptophan → serotonin → melatonin. Glutamate → GABA (glutamate decarboxylase, B6, which is why B6 deficiency from isoniazid can cause seizures). Histidine → histamine. Choline + acetyl-CoA → acetylcholine (choline acetyltransferase).']]},
{h:'The barrier and the fluid',p:[
'The **blood-brain barrier** has three layers: tight junctions between non-fenestrated capillary endothelial cells, a basement membrane, and astrocyte foot processes. Small and lipid-soluble molecules cross freely (O₂, CO₂, alcohol, most anaesthetics, heroin faster than morphine); glucose and amino acids need carriers; ionised and protein-bound drugs stay out. It is absent at the **circumventricular organs**, which must sample the blood: area postrema (the vomiting centre, which senses toxins and chemotherapy), OVLT (osmolality), subfornical organ (angiotensin II, thirst), posterior pituitary and median eminence. Infection, tumours, radiation and severe hypertension break the barrier and let fluid out (**vasogenic oedema**, which steroids reduce); hypoxia swells the cells themselves (**cytotoxic oedema**, which steroids do not help).',
'**CSF** (about 150 mL at any time, made at about 500 mL a day, mostly by the choroid plexus) flows: lateral ventricles → interventricular foramina of Monro → third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → lateral foramina of Luschka and midline foramen of Magendie → subarachnoid space → absorbed by **arachnoid granulations** into the dural venous sinuses. Normal CSF is clear, with opening pressure up to about 20 cm H₂O, fewer than 5 white cells per µL (all lymphocytes), protein under 0.45 g/L, and glucose about two thirds of the blood level.'],
table:{cols:['Hydrocephalus','Cause and clues'],rows:[
['Communicating','absorption fails at the arachnoid granulations: after meningitis or subarachnoid haemorrhage; all ventricles enlarge'],
['Non-communicating (obstructive)','flow blocked inside the ventricles: aqueductal stenosis, colloid cyst of the third ventricle, posterior fossa tumour, Chiari II, Dandy-Walker'],
['Normal pressure','older adults; enlarged ventricles with normal opening pressure; gait goes first (magnetic, feet stuck to the floor), then urinary urgency and incontinence, then dementia; improves after a large-volume lumbar tap; ventriculoperitoneal shunt'],
['Ex vacuo','ventricles look large because brain tissue is lost (Alzheimer, Huntington, HIV); pressure normal; not true hydrocephalus'],
['Idiopathic intracranial hypertension','young women with obesity; headache, papilloedema, pulsatile tinnitus, CN VI palsy, vision loss; normal imaging, high opening pressure; weight loss, acetazolamide; triggered by vitamin A excess, tetracyclines, isotretinoin']]},
key:'Normal pressure hydrocephalus unfolds from the floor up: feet first, then bladder, then mind.',
s:[['pharm','Lipid solubility is the passport across the barrier, and anaesthetics show it best. The more lipid-soluble an inhaled anaesthetic, the more potent it is (lower minimum alveolar concentration, MAC); the less soluble it is in blood (low blood:gas partition coefficient), the faster induction and recovery are, so nitrous oxide works fast and is weak, while halothane is slow and strong. The full anaesthetic pharmacology is in the Sleeping Draughts.']]},
{h:'Meningitis and encephalitis',p:[
'**Bacterial meningitis**: fever, headache, neck stiffness and altered mental state (all three in under half of patients), photophobia, seizures; a non-blanching petechial or purpuric rash means meningococcus until proven otherwise. Infants show only fever, irritability, poor feeding and a bulging fontanelle. Take blood cultures and give antibiotics at once; scan before lumbar puncture only if there are focal signs, papilloedema, seizures, reduced consciousness or immunosuppression, and never let the scan delay treatment. Dexamethasone given with the first dose reduces hearing loss and death in pneumococcal meningitis. Close contacts of meningococcal disease receive prophylaxis (ciprofloxacin, rifampicin or ceftriaxone).',
'CSF sorts the cause. **Bacterial**: high opening pressure, hundreds to thousands of neutrophils, high protein, glucose under 40% of blood. **Viral** (enteroviruses commonest, HSV-2, VZV, HIV, mumps): lymphocytes, mildly raised protein, normal glucose. **Tuberculous and fungal**: lymphocytes, very high protein, low glucose; TB favours the basal meninges and cranial nerves; *Cryptococcus* (AIDS, CD4 under 100) shows a high opening pressure, India ink capsules and a positive antigen test. Complications: deafness (the commonest lasting one, especially in children), hydrocephalus, cranial nerve palsies, seizures, and Waterhouse-Friderichsen adrenal haemorrhage with meningococcaemia.'],
table:{cols:['Age','Organisms','Empirical therapy'],rows:[['Neonate (< 1 month)','group B streptococcus, E. coli, Listeria','ampicillin + cefotaxime (or gentamicin)'],['1 month – 50 years','S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae (unvaccinated)','ceftriaxone + vancomycin (+ dexamethasone)'],['> 50 years or immunocompromised','S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, Listeria, Gram-negatives','ceftriaxone + vancomycin + ampicillin']]},
s:[['micro','**HSV-1 encephalitis**: fever, personality change, aphasia, seizures and smells that are not there, from haemorrhagic necrosis of the temporal lobes; CSF PCR, MRI temporal signal, periodic discharges on EEG; IV aciclovir immediately, on suspicion. **Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy**: JC virus kills oligodendrocytes in AIDS or with natalizumab and rituximab; patchy non-enhancing white-matter lesions. **Brain abscess**: streptococci (from sinuses, teeth, endocarditis), staphylococci (trauma, surgery), anaerobes; ring-enhancing. In AIDS, ring-enhancing lesions are *Toxoplasma* (multiple, basal ganglia; positive serology) or primary CNS lymphoma (often single, EBV in CSF).']]},
{h:'The keepers of the inner rooms',p:[
'The **hypothalamus**, below the thalamus and above the pituitary, keeps the body\'s settings: temperature, hunger, thirst, water balance, circadian time, and the pituitary through releasing hormones (into the portal vessels) and through ADH and oxytocin (carried down axons to the posterior pituitary). Lesions here come from craniopharyngioma, sarcoidosis, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, trauma and radiation; the tumours also press on the chiasm above.',
'The **thalamus** is the relay for almost every sensation on its way to the cortex (smell is the exception) and loops motor signals from the basal ganglia and cerebellum back to the motor cortex. The **limbic system** handles emotion, motivation, memory and the drives that keep a creature alive: feeding, fight and flight, mating. Its core: the **hippocampus** (forming new long-term memories), **amygdala** (fear, emotional memory), **mammillary bodies** and the **fornix** that joins them, the anterior thalamic nucleus and the **cingulate gyrus**, all linked in a circuit (the Papez circuit).'],
table:{cols:['Structure','Job','When damaged or driven'],rows:[
['Lateral hypothalamus','hunger (orexin neurons; leptin inhibits it)','destroyed: anorexia, wasting; orexin loss: narcolepsy'],
['Ventromedial hypothalamus','satiety (leptin stimulates it)','destroyed: hyperphagia and obesity (craniopharyngioma)'],
['Anterior hypothalamus','heat loss, parasympathetic tone','destroyed: hyperthermia'],
['Posterior hypothalamus','heat conservation, sympathetic tone','destroyed: poikilothermia (body takes the room temperature)'],
['Suprachiasmatic nucleus','circadian clock, drives pineal melatonin','sleep–wake rhythm lost'],
['Supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei','make ADH and oxytocin; paraventricular also CRH and TRH','central diabetes insipidus'],
['Arcuate nucleus','dopamine (inhibits prolactin), GHRH','stalk compression: prolactin rises'],
['Preoptic area','GnRH, temperature set point, sexual behaviour','GnRH neurons that never migrated there: Kallmann syndrome (with anosmia)'],
['Thalamus: VPL','body pain, temperature, touch, proprioception','contralateral hemisensory loss; later thalamic pain'],
['Thalamus: VPM','face sensation and taste','contralateral facial numbness'],
['Thalamus: LGN / MGN','vision / hearing','contralateral homonymous hemianopia / little hearing loss (above the cochlear nuclei each ear projects to both sides)'],
['Thalamus: VL, VA','motor loops from cerebellum and basal ganglia','contralateral ataxia, tremor'],
['Thalamus: medial dorsal, anterior','memory (with the mammillary bodies)','amnesia (Korsakoff)'],
['Amygdala (both sides)','fear, emotional tagging of memory','Klüver-Bucy: placidity, hyperorality, hypersexuality, visual agnosia; HSV-1 encephalitis'],
['Hippocampus (both sides)','new declarative memory','anterograde amnesia; most vulnerable to hypoxia']]},
s:[['phys','Leptin, from fat, tells the hypothalamus that stores are full: it stimulates satiety and suppresses hunger. Ghrelin, from the stomach, rises before meals and drives hunger. Leptin deficiency or receptor mutations cause severe early obesity; in common obesity leptin is high but the brain resists it.']]},
{h:'Where the tube closed',p:[
'In week 3 the notochord induces the overlying ectoderm to thicken into the **neural plate**. Its edges fold up and fuse into the **neural tube** during week 4: the cranial end (anterior neuropore) closes around day 25, the caudal end (posterior neuropore) around day 27–28, usually before a pregnancy is recognised, which is why folic acid must start before conception. The tube makes the CNS (neurons, oligodendrocytes, astrocytes, ependyma), the retina, pineal gland and posterior pituitary. **Neural crest** cells pinched off its edges wander widely: dorsal root and autonomic ganglia, Schwann cells, melanocytes, adrenal medullary chromaffin cells, parafollicular C cells, the pia and arachnoid, odontoblasts, skull and face bones, and the aorticopulmonary septum. Microglia come from yolk-sac macrophages, not from the tube.',
'Failures of closure are **neural tube defects**: risk rises with folate deficiency, valproate or carbamazepine, maternal diabetes and obesity. Open defects leak **AFP** and **acetylcholinesterase** into the amniotic fluid and maternal serum. The forebrain must also split in two (driven by **sonic hedgehog**); when it does not, the result is **holoprosencephaly**.'],
table:{cols:['Malformation','What went wrong','Clue'],rows:[
['Anencephaly','anterior neuropore did not close','no forebrain or skull vault; very high AFP; polyhydramnios (fetus cannot swallow)'],
['Spina bifida occulta','vertebral arch did not close; nothing herniates','tuft of hair or dimple over the low back; normal AFP'],
['Meningocele','meninges herniate through the gap','sac with no cord tissue; AFP may rise'],
['Myelomeningocele','meninges and cord herniate','raised AFP and amniotic acetylcholinesterase; leg weakness, neurogenic bladder; with Chiari II and hydrocephalus'],
['Holoprosencephaly','forebrain did not divide (SHH pathway)','Patau syndrome, fetal alcohol; single central incisor, cleft lip, cyclopia in severe forms'],
['Chiari I','cerebellar tonsils sit below the foramen magnum','adults; headache worse with coughing; syringomyelia'],
['Chiari II','cerebellar vermis and tonsils herniate through the foramen magnum','lumbar myelomeningocele, obstructive hydrocephalus'],
['Dandy-Walker','vermis absent or tiny, cystic fourth ventricle filling an enlarged posterior fossa','hydrocephalus in infancy']]}}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 25-year-old has fever and neck stiffness; CSF shows neutrophils, high protein and low glucose. Empirical therapy?',o:['Aciclovir and close observation','Ampicillin alone','Amphotericin B','Ceftriaxone plus vancomycin (and dexamethasone)'],a:3,d:'micro',c:'meningitis',x:'In adults under 50 the main organisms are pneumococcus and meningococcus, covered by ceftriaxone, with vancomycin for resistant pneumococci and dexamethasone added. Aciclovir is for HSV encephalitis, which gives a lymphocytic CSF with normal glucose.'},
{q:'A 72-year-old has a magnetic gait, then urinary urgency, then forgetfulness; the ventricles are large and the opening pressure is normal. Diagnosis?',o:['Normal pressure hydrocephalus','Alzheimer disease with cerebral atrophy','Parkinson disease','Hydrocephalus ex vacuo'],a:0,d:'path',c:'dementia',x:'Gait first, then bladder, then cognition with large ventricles is normal pressure hydrocephalus, and it may improve after a large-volume tap. Ex vacuo enlargement also shows big ventricles, but the sulci are wide from atrophy and there is no gait-first triad.'},
{q:'Which glial cell myelinates many CNS axons at once and is the target in multiple sclerosis?',o:['Schwann cell','Fibrous astrocyte','Oligodendrocyte','Microglia'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'multiple-sclerosis',x:'One oligodendrocyte myelinates segments of many CNS axons, and these are the cells injured in MS and PML. Schwann cells myelinate a single peripheral segment and are the target in Guillain-Barré syndrome.'},
{q:'Where is the blood-brain barrier absent so that blood-borne toxins can trigger vomiting?',o:['Hippocampus','Area postrema','Thalamus','Cerebellar vermis'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'excitability',x:'The area postrema in the floor of the fourth ventricle is a circumventricular organ: its leaky capillaries let it sense emetic drugs and toxins. The other structures sit behind an intact barrier.'},
{q:'A 10-day-old has meningitis. Which drug must be included to cover Listeria?',o:['Ampicillin','Ceftriaxone','Vancomycin','Gentamicin alone'],a:0,d:'micro',c:'meningitis',x:'Listeria is intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins, so ampicillin is added in neonates, adults over 50 and the immunocompromised. Gentamicin works with ampicillin synergistically but is not adequate on its own.'},
{q:'After surgery for a suprasellar craniopharyngioma, a boy eats constantly and gains weight rapidly. Which nucleus was damaged?',o:['Lateral hypothalamus','Ventromedial hypothalamus','Suprachiasmatic nucleus (clock)','Posterior hypothalamus'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'pituitary',x:'The ventromedial nucleus is the satiety centre, and its destruction removes the signal to stop eating. Destroying the lateral nucleus does the opposite, causing anorexia and wasting.'},
{q:'Raised AFP and acetylcholinesterase in amniotic fluid; ultrasound shows a lumbar sac containing cord and nerve roots. Diagnosis?',o:['Spina bifida occulta','Meningocele','Anencephaly','Myelomeningocele'],a:3,d:'path',c:'b12-folate',x:'A sac that holds neural tissue is a myelomeningocele, an open defect that leaks AFP and acetylcholinesterase and usually comes with Chiari II. A meningocele contains only meninges, and spina bifida occulta has no sac and a normal AFP.'}
]};

BOOK['c16-tracts']={depth:16,title:'The Long Roads',
epi:'Long roads run from the abyss to every part of the body. A single break and a whole region goes dark.',
c:['spinal-cord','b12-folate','vitamins','repeat-expansion'],
S:[
{h:'Three tracts',p:[
'**Dorsal columns–medial lemniscus** (fine touch, vibration, proprioception): the first-order neuron sits in the dorsal root ganglion and its axon climbs the **same side** of the cord, in the fasciculus gracilis (legs, medial) or the fasciculus cuneatus (arms, lateral, above T6). It synapses in the nucleus gracilis or cuneatus in the lower medulla; second-order fibres **cross in the medulla** (internal arcuate fibres) and rise as the medial lemniscus to the **VPL** of the thalamus, then to the postcentral gyrus.',
'**Spinothalamic tract** (pain, temperature, crude touch): the first-order neuron enters through the dorsal root and synapses in the dorsal horn; the second-order fibres **cross within 1–2 segments in the anterior white commissure**, then ascend contralaterally in the anterolateral cord to the VPL. So a cord lesion on one side removes pain and temperature on the opposite side, starting one or two segments below the lesion.',
'**Lateral corticospinal tract** (voluntary movement): upper motor neurons in the precentral gyrus send axons through the posterior limb of the **internal capsule**, the cerebral peduncle and the basis pontis to the medullary **pyramids**, where about 85–90% **cross**. They descend in the contralateral lateral column to synapse on anterior horn lower motor neurons. Inside the cord both the corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts carry the arms nearest the centre and the legs at the edge, which is why a lesion of the cord\'s centre hits the arms first.'],
table:{cols:['Sign','Upper motor neuron','Lower motor neuron'],rows:[['Tone','↑ (spastic, clasp-knife)','↓ (flaccid)'],['Reflexes','↑, with clonus','↓ or absent'],['Plantar response','up-going (Babinski)','down-going'],['Wasting','mild, late (disuse)','marked, early'],['Fasciculations','no','yes'],['Weakness pattern','arm extensors and leg flexors weakest (pyramidal)','follows the nerve or root']]},
key:'Reflex roots: Achilles S1, patellar L3–4, biceps and brachioradialis C5–6, triceps C7. Cremasteric L1–2; anal wink S2–4.',
s:[['phys','Right after an acute cord injury the limbs below are flaccid and areflexic (**spinal shock**), lasting hours to weeks, before spasticity and brisk reflexes emerge. Above T6, a full bladder or bowel below the lesion can trigger **autonomic dysreflexia**: surging blood pressure with reflex bradycardia and flushing above the level; sit the patient up and remove the trigger.']]},
{h:'Cord lesions',fig:'cord',p:[
'Patterns follow the tracts. Three questions localise a cord lesion. Is there a **level**, a line on the trunk below which sensation or strength changes? Which tracts are lost, on which side? Are the lower motor neurons at that level also damaged (wasting, lost reflexes at the level, brisk ones below)? Cranial nerves are always normal in a pure cord lesion.',
'Two sites deserve special care. A **conus medullaris** lesion (around L1–L2) gives early, symmetric bladder and bowel failure, saddle anaesthesia and a mix of upper and lower motor neuron signs. A **cauda equina** lesion (roots below L2) gives asymmetric, painful, purely lower motor neuron weakness with saddle anaesthesia and later sphincter failure. Lesions above the sacral centre leave a **spastic bladder** (urgency, small volumes); lesions of the sacral cord or roots leave a **flaccid bladder** (retention, overflow).'],
table:{cols:['Lesion','Cause','Findings'],rows:[
['Brown-Séquard (hemisection)','trauma, tumour, disc','ipsilateral weakness (UMN below, LMN at the level) and loss of vibration/proprioception below; contralateral pain and temperature loss from 1–2 levels below'],
['Central cord syndrome','neck hyperextension in an older person with cervical spondylosis; syrinx','arms weaker than legs, hands worst; patchy sensory loss below; bladder retention'],
['Syringomyelia','Chiari I, trauma, tumour','cape-like loss of pain and temperature over both shoulders and arms (crossing fibres), touch preserved; later wasting of hand muscles, then spastic legs'],
['Subacute combined degeneration','B12 deficiency, nitrous oxide abuse; copper deficiency mimics it (bariatric surgery, excess zinc)','dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts: sensory ataxia, lost vibration, spastic weakness, up-going plantars; often with neuropathy'],
['Tabes dorsalis','tertiary syphilis','dorsal roots and columns: sensory ataxia, Romberg +, lightning pains, Argyll Robertson pupils, Charcot joints, absent reflexes'],
['Anterior spinal artery infarct','aortic aneurysm repair, dissection, hypotension','anterior two thirds lost: paralysis and pain/temperature loss below; dorsal columns spared'],
['Transverse myelitis','after infection, MS, neuromyelitis optica (aquaporin-4 antibodies)','bilateral weakness, sensory level and sphincter failure over hours to days'],
['ALS','sporadic; familial SOD1, C9orf72','upper and lower motor neuron signs, no sensory loss, eye movements and sphincters spared; riluzole (↓ glutamate), edaravone (antioxidant), tofersen (antisense, SOD1 only)'],
['Spinal muscular atrophy','SMN1 deletion (autosomal recessive)','anterior horn loss: floppy infant, tongue fasciculations; nusinersen (antisense), risdiplam (oral), onasemnogene abeparvovec (gene therapy)'],
['Friedreich ataxia','GAA repeat in FXN (frataxin; autosomal recessive)','dorsal columns, spinocerebellar and corticospinal tracts: ataxia from childhood, absent reflexes with up-going plantars, pes cavus, scoliosis; hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (main cause of death), diabetes'],
['Vitamin E deficiency','fat malabsorption, cystic fibrosis, abetalipoproteinaemia','dorsal columns and spinocerebellar tracts, neuropathy, haemolysis; looks like Friedreich ataxia'],
['Poliomyelitis','poliovirus (anterior horn)','asymmetric flaccid weakness, no sensory loss'],
['Cauda equina','central disc prolapse, tumour, haematoma','saddle anaesthesia, bowel and bladder dysfunction, absent ankle reflexes; emergency MRI and decompression']]}},
{h:'Peripheral nerves',p:[
'**Guillain-Barré syndrome** (acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy): symmetric weakness rising from the legs over days to 4 weeks, areflexia, mild sensory symptoms, facial weakness, autonomic instability (arrhythmia, swinging blood pressure). It follows *Campylobacter jejuni*, CMV, EBV or other infections through **molecular mimicry**: antibodies against microbial sugars cross-react with gangliosides on nerve. CSF shows **albuminocytological dissociation** (high protein, normal cells); nerve conduction is slowed. Treat with **IVIG or plasma exchange** (steroids do not help) and watch the forced vital capacity: falling toward 20 mL/kg means intubation. Variants: axonal forms (anti-GM1) and **Miller Fisher syndrome** (ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia; anti-GQ1b). When the same picture creeps on beyond 8 weeks it is **CIDP**, which does respond to steroids.',
'**Diabetic neuropathy**: length-dependent, symmetric stocking-glove sensory loss first (painful or numb feet, ulcers), plus autonomic neuropathy (gastroparesis, orthostasis, erectile dysfunction) and mononeuropathies (CN III sparing the pupil). Other causes of a symmetric neuropathy: alcohol, B12, B1 and B6 deficiency, B6 excess, drugs (isoniazid, vincristine, paclitaxel, cisplatin, metronidazole), HIV, leprosy, hypothyroidism, amyloid, paraproteins. **Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease** (most often a PMP22 duplication, autosomal dominant): hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy with pes cavus, hammer toes, distal wasting ("inverted champagne bottle" legs).'],
table:{cols:['Nerve injured','Cause','Result'],rows:[
['Upper trunk (C5–C6)','fall on the shoulder, traction at birth (Erb palsy)','arm hangs at the side, internally rotated, elbow straight, wrist flexed ("waiter\'s tip")'],
['Lower trunk (C8–T1)','grabbing a branch while falling, birth (Klumpke palsy), Pancoast tumour','claw hand; Horner syndrome if T1 is involved'],
['Axillary','surgical neck of humerus fracture, anterior shoulder dislocation','weak abduction beyond 15° (deltoid), numb patch over the deltoid'],
['Musculocutaneous','upper arm trauma','weak elbow flexion and supination, numb lateral forearm'],
['Radial','midshaft humerus fracture (spiral groove), crutches, sleeping with the arm over a chair','wrist drop, weak finger extension, numb dorsum of the hand; triceps spared in spiral groove injuries'],
['Median','supracondylar fracture (high), carpal tunnel (low)','high: cannot flex the index and middle fingers ("hand of benediction" on making a fist); low: thenar wasting, numb lateral 3½ fingers, Tinel and Phalen signs'],
['Ulnar','medial epicondyle fracture, leaning on the elbow, hook of hamate (cyclists)','claw of the 4th and 5th fingers (worse with distal lesions), weak finger spreading, numb medial 1½ fingers, Froment sign'],
['Long thoracic','mastectomy, axillary node dissection','winged scapula (serratus anterior)'],
['Superior gluteal','misplaced buttock injection, hip surgery','Trendelenburg sign: standing on the injured leg, the pelvis drops on the opposite side (gluteus medius and minimus)'],
['Inferior gluteal','posterior hip dislocation, hip surgery','weak hip extension (gluteus maximus): trouble climbing stairs or rising from a chair'],
['Femoral','pelvic fracture, femoral catheter, iliopsoas haematoma','weak knee extension, lost patellar reflex, numb anterior thigh and medial leg'],
['Obturator','pelvic surgery, anterior hip dislocation','weak thigh adduction, numb medial thigh'],
['Tibial','knee trauma, Baker cyst, tarsal tunnel','cannot plantarflex or invert, cannot stand on tiptoe, numb sole'],
['Common fibular','fibular neck fracture, crossing the legs, tight casts','foot drop (cannot dorsiflex or evert), high-stepping gait, numb dorsum of the foot']]},
s:[['pharm','**Local anaesthetics** cross the nerve membrane in their uncharged form, then block voltage-gated Na⁺ channels from inside in their charged form, binding best to open channels (use dependence). Small myelinated fibres block first, then small unmyelinated ones, then large myelinated ones: pain and temperature go before touch, pressure and motor power. Acidic, infected tissue keeps more drug charged outside the nerve, so the block is weaker. **Esters** (procaine, tetracaine, benzocaine, cocaine) are broken down by plasma cholinesterase to PABA, which causes most allergies; benzocaine can cause methaemoglobinaemia. **Amides** (lidocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine) are cleared by the liver. Toxicity starts in the CNS (perioral tingling, tinnitus, seizures) and then the heart (arrhythmia, collapse; bupivacaine worst): treat with **lipid emulsion**. Cocaine is the only one that constricts vessels on its own; others are often given with adrenaline. General anaesthesia and neuromuscular blockers are in the Sleeping Draughts.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'After a stab wound to the right half of the cord at T10, which deficit appears on the left from about T11–T12 down?',o:['Spastic weakness of the leg','Loss of vibration and joint position','Loss of pain and temperature','Up-going plantar'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'spinal-cord',x:'Spinothalamic fibres cross within 1–2 segments of entry, so a right hemisection removes pain and temperature on the left from just below the lesion. Weakness, vibration loss and the up-going plantar all appear on the right, the same side as the cut, because those tracts cross in the medulla.'},
{q:'Cape-like loss of pain and temperature over both shoulders and arms with touch preserved. Diagnosis?',o:['Brown-Séquard syndrome','Tabes dorsalis','Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis','Syringomyelia'],a:3,d:'path',c:'spinal-cord',x:'A central cervical syrinx first cuts the spinothalamic fibres crossing in the anterior white commissure, giving bilateral, suspended pain and temperature loss. Tabes dorsalis affects the dorsal columns and roots, so touch, vibration and position sense are lost instead.'},
{q:'Ascending weakness and areflexia two weeks after Campylobacter enteritis. Typical CSF?',o:['Raised protein with a normal cell count','Raised cells and raised protein','Low glucose','Oligoclonal bands absent from serum'],a:0,d:'path',x:'Guillain-Barré syndrome shows albuminocytological dissociation: protein rises from inflamed roots while the white cell count stays normal. A raised cell count with raised protein suggests infection, or an HIV-related polyradiculopathy, rather than GBS.'},
{q:'Wrist drop and numbness over the back of the hand after a midshaft humerus fracture. Nerve?',o:['Median','Ulnar','Axillary','Radial'],a:3,d:'phys',x:'The radial nerve winds around the humerus in the spiral groove, so a midshaft fracture injures it, causing wrist drop with triceps usually spared. The axillary nerve is hurt by fractures of the surgical neck, higher up.'},
{q:'Mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs in the limbs and tongue, with normal sensation and normal eye movements. Diagnosis and drug?',o:['Multiple sclerosis; interferon-β injections','Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis; riluzole','Myasthenia gravis; pyridostigmine','Guillain-Barré syndrome; IVIG'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'spinal-cord',x:'ALS destroys upper and lower motor neurons while sparing sensation, eye movements and sphincters; riluzole reduces glutamate release and modestly prolongs survival. Myasthenia gravis tempts with bulbar weakness, but it gives fatigable weakness with normal reflexes and no fasciculations.'},
{q:'A man who inhales nitrous oxide recreationally develops unsteady gait, loss of vibration sense, brisk knee reflexes and up-going plantars. Diagnosis?',o:['Tabes dorsalis','Subacute combined degeneration of the cord','Anterior spinal artery infarct','Friedreich ataxia'],a:1,d:'bio',c:'b12-folate',x:'Nitrous oxide oxidises the cobalt in B12 and inactivates methionine synthase, damaging the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts. Tabes dorsalis also removes vibration sense but spares the corticospinal tracts, so the reflexes are lost rather than brisk.'},
{q:'After a buttock injection, when the patient stands on her right leg the left side of her pelvis drops. Injured nerve?',o:['Right superior gluteal','Left superior gluteal','Right inferior gluteal','Right femoral'],a:0,d:'phys',x:'The superior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus medius and minimus, which hold the pelvis level when standing on that leg; weakness on the right lets the left side drop (Trendelenburg sign). The tempting left nerve is wrong because the dropping side is opposite to the weak muscles.'}
]};

BOOK['c16-stroke']={depth:16,title:'The Dark Territories',
epi:'Whole territories of the abyss have gone dark where the blood stopped reaching them.',
c:['stroke','atherosclerosis','hypertension','coagulation'],
S:[
{h:'Arterial territories',p:[
'Two circulations feed the brain. The **anterior** comes from the internal carotids, which become the anterior and middle cerebral arteries; the **posterior** comes from the vertebrals, which join as the basilar and end as the posterior cerebral arteries. The circle of Willis links them. Ischaemic stroke (about 85% of strokes) comes from **large-artery atherosclerosis** (carotid bifurcation, with artery-to-artery emboli), **cardioembolism** (atrial fibrillation, recent MI with mural thrombus, endocarditis, a patent foramen ovale letting a venous clot cross), **small-vessel disease** (lacunes), or other causes (arterial dissection in the young, vasculitis, sickle cell disease, antiphospholipid syndrome).',
'**Middle cerebral artery**: contralateral face and arm weakness and sensory loss more than leg, gaze deviation toward the lesion, contralateral homonymous hemianopia, and **aphasia** if the dominant (usually left) hemisphere is hit or **hemineglect** if the non-dominant (usually right) parietal lobe is. **Anterior cerebral artery**: contralateral leg and foot weakness and sensory loss, urinary incontinence, apathy. **Posterior cerebral artery**: contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing; a dominant PCA stroke that also takes the splenium causes alexia without agraphia. **Lacunar strokes** (lipohyalinosis or microatheroma of small penetrating arteries; hypertension, diabetes): **pure motor** (posterior limb of the internal capsule, lenticulostriate branches of the MCA), **pure sensory** (thalamic VPL, thalamoperforating and thalamogeniculate branches of the PCA), ataxic hemiparesis, and dysarthria–clumsy hand. A lacune never causes aphasia, neglect or a field cut; those are cortical signs and mean a larger vessel.',
'A **transient ischaemic attack** is a brief focal deficit (usually minutes, under an hour) with no infarct on imaging. It carries a high early stroke risk, so it needs urgent work-up within 24 hours: carotid imaging, ECG and rhythm monitoring, glucose and lipids. **Amaurosis fugax** (a curtain over one eye for minutes) is a TIA of the retinal circulation, usually an embolus from the ipsilateral carotid.'],
table:{cols:['Artery','Territory','Deficit'],rows:[
['ACA','medial frontal and parietal cortex','contralateral leg and foot weakness and numbness; incontinence, apathy'],
['MCA, superior division','lateral frontal lobe, Broca area, face and arm motor strip','contralateral face and arm weakness; Broca aphasia (dominant); eyes deviate toward the lesion'],
['MCA, inferior division','lateral temporal and parietal lobes, Wernicke area','Wernicke aphasia (dominant) or neglect (non-dominant); contralateral upper quadrantanopia; little weakness'],
['Lenticulostriate (MCA)','internal capsule, basal ganglia','pure motor hemiparesis, dysarthria–clumsy hand, ataxic hemiparesis'],
['Thalamoperforating, thalamogeniculate (PCA)','thalamus','pure sensory stroke (VPL); later a burning thalamic pain (Dejerine-Roussy)'],
['PCA','occipital cortex, medial temporal lobe','contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing; alexia without agraphia; memory loss'],
['Paramedian PCA branches','midbrain: cerebral peduncle, CN III fascicle','Weber syndrome: ipsilateral CN III palsy, contralateral hemiparesis'],
['Basilar, paramedian branches','ventral pons','one side: contralateral hemiparesis with ipsilateral CN VI palsy; both sides: locked-in syndrome'],
['AICA','lateral lower pons, inner ear','ipsilateral facial paralysis, deafness, vertigo, facial pain/temperature loss and ataxia; contralateral body pain/temperature loss'],
['PICA (or vertebral)','lateral medulla','Wallenberg: vertigo, nystagmus, dysphagia and hoarseness (nucleus ambiguus), ipsilateral face and contralateral body pain/temperature loss, ipsilateral Horner and ataxia; no limb weakness'],
['Anterior spinal / vertebral','medial medulla: pyramid, medial lemniscus, CN XII','contralateral hemiparesis and loss of vibration/proprioception; tongue deviates toward the lesion']]},
key:'Crossed signs (a cranial nerve on one side, the body on the other) put the lesion in the brainstem, on the side of the cranial nerve. Facial paralysis with deafness: AICA. Hoarseness and swallowing trouble: PICA.',
s:[['pharm','**Acute ischaemic stroke**: first a CT to exclude bleeding. Thrombolysis with **alteplase or tenecteplase** within 4.5 hours of onset (or of last seen well) if there is no haemorrhage and no contraindication; blood pressure must be below 185/110 before and 180/105 for 24 hours after. **Mechanical thrombectomy** for a large-vessel occlusion, up to 24 hours in selected patients with salvageable tissue on imaging. Aspirin within 48 hours (24 hours after lysis); for a minor stroke or high-risk TIA, aspirin plus clopidogrel for about 21 days, then one antiplatelet. Secondary prevention: high-intensity statin, blood pressure control, a DOAC for atrial fibrillation, carotid endarterectomy or stenting for symptomatic stenosis of about 70–99% (considered from 50%), stopping smoking.']]},
{h:'The broken languages',p:[
'Language lives in the **dominant hemisphere**, the left in about 95% of right-handed people and most left-handed ones. Two areas and a cable carry it. **Broca area** (posterior inferior frontal gyrus) builds speech; **Wernicke area** (posterior superior temporal gyrus) gives words their meaning; the **arcuate fasciculus** joins them. Aphasia is a disorder of language (words, grammar, meaning), unlike **dysarthria**, which is a disorder of the muscles of speech with normal language.',
'Three tests at the bedside sort the aphasias: is the speech **fluent**, is it **understood**, can the patient **repeat** a phrase? **Broca aphasia**: halting, effortful, telegraphic speech with good comprehension and poor repetition; the patient knows and is frustrated, and the nearby motor strip is often hit too (right face and arm weakness). **Wernicke aphasia**: fluent, well-articulated speech full of wrong and invented words, poor comprehension and repetition; the patient often seems unaware, and there may be no weakness at all, so it can be mistaken for confusion or psychosis. **Conduction aphasia** (arcuate fasciculus, supramarginal gyrus): fluent speech with sound errors and good comprehension, but repetition is strikingly poor. **Global aphasia**: everything lost, from a large MCA stroke. The **transcortical aphasias** come from watershed infarcts that spare Broca, Wernicke and the arcuate but cut them off from the rest of the brain: speech or comprehension fails, yet repetition is preserved, sometimes as echoing. **Anomic aphasia**: fluent speech with word-finding pauses only; it is the mildest form, seen with many lesions and early Alzheimer disease.'],
table:{cols:['Aphasia (site)','Speech','Comprehension','Repetition'],rows:[
['Broca (dominant inferior frontal gyrus)','non-fluent, effortful','intact','poor'],
['Wernicke (dominant superior temporal gyrus)','fluent, meaningless','poor','poor'],
['Conduction (arcuate fasciculus)','fluent, sound errors','intact','very poor'],
['Global (large MCA territory)','non-fluent or mute','poor','poor'],
['Transcortical motor (frontal watershed)','non-fluent','intact','intact'],
['Transcortical sensory (temporoparietal watershed)','fluent','poor','intact, may echo'],
['Mixed transcortical (both watersheds)','non-fluent','poor','intact'],
['Anomic (many sites)','fluent, word-finding pauses','intact','intact']]},
key:'If the patient can repeat, the arcuate road is open: think transcortical or anomic. If only repetition fails, the road itself is cut: conduction aphasia.'},
{h:'Where the damage is',p:[
'Localising a lesion starts with the level. **Cortex**: aphasia, neglect, a visual field cut, cortical sensory loss (cannot recognise an object by touch), seizures, face and arm affected more than leg or the reverse. **Subcortical white matter and internal capsule**: dense face, arm and leg weakness together, with no cortical signs. **Brainstem**: crossed signs, diplopia, vertigo, dysarthria and dysphagia, a cranial nerve palsy on the side of the lesion with body signs on the other. **Cerebellum**: ataxia on the side of the lesion. **Cord**: a sensory level, no cranial nerve signs. **Peripheral nerve or root**: lower motor neuron signs in one territory. The cranial nerves and their nuclei, rule by rule, are in the Twelve Wires.'],
table:{cols:['Damaged structure','Deficit'],rows:[
['Frontal lobe (prefrontal)','disinhibition, poor judgement and planning, apathy, primitive reflexes (grasp, rooting), personality change'],
['Frontal eye field','destructive lesion: eyes look toward the lesion; seizure focus: eyes driven away from it'],
['Dominant parietal (angular gyrus)','Gerstmann syndrome: agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, left-right confusion'],
['Non-dominant parietal','hemispatial neglect of the opposite side, dressing and constructional apraxia, denial of weakness'],
['Bilateral occipital','cortical blindness; denial of it (Anton syndrome)'],
['Bilateral amygdala','Klüver-Bucy syndrome: hyperorality, hypersexuality, placidity, visual agnosia (HSV-1 encephalitis)'],
['Bilateral hippocampus','anterograde amnesia (HSV-1, hypoxia)'],
['Mammillary bodies, medial thalamus','Wernicke-Korsakoff: confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, then amnesia with confabulation'],
['Subthalamic nucleus','contralateral hemiballismus (flinging movements)'],
['Dorsal midbrain (pineal tumour)','Parinaud syndrome: upgaze palsy, pupils react to near but not light, convergence-retraction nystagmus, lid retraction'],
['PPRF (pons)','cannot look toward the side of the lesion; eyes deviate away from it'],
['MLF','internuclear ophthalmoplegia: the eye on the side of the lesion fails to adduct, the other eye shows nystagmus on abduction'],
['Cerebellar hemisphere','ipsilateral limb ataxia, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia, falls toward the lesion'],
['Cerebellar vermis','truncal ataxia, wide-based gait, dysarthria (alcohol)'],
['Reticular activating system (upper brainstem)','reduced consciousness, coma']]},
s:[['phys','Which way do the eyes look? A destroyed frontal eye field lets the healthy one push the eyes toward the damaged side, so after a large right hemisphere stroke the eyes look right, away from the weak left limbs. A seizure does the opposite and drives the eyes away from the irritated side. A pontine (PPRF) lesion reverses the rule: the eyes look away from the lesion and toward the weak side.']]},
{h:'Bleeding in the head',p:[
'Where the blood is tells you what bled. **Haemorrhagic stroke** (about 15%) is either **intracerebral** (into the brain) or **subarachnoid**. Hypertensive intracerebral bleeds come from Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms of the small penetrating arteries, in the basal ganglia (putamen most often), thalamus, pons and cerebellum. **Cerebral amyloid angiopathy** (β-amyloid in the walls of cortical vessels) causes recurrent lobar bleeds and cortical microbleeds in older people. Other causes: anticoagulants, AVMs, tumours, cocaine and amphetamines.',
'**Subarachnoid haemorrhage**: a sudden, maximal "thunderclap" headache, vomiting, neck stiffness, sometimes collapse. CT within 6 hours of onset is almost always positive; after that, lumbar puncture at 12 hours or more shows xanthochromia (bilirubin from broken-down red cells). Most are from a ruptured **berry aneurysm** at a circle of Willis branch point (anterior communicating artery commonest; posterior communicating can press CN III); risk with ADPKD, Ehlers-Danlos, Marfan, coarctation, smoking and hypertension. The aneurysm is secured early by coiling or clipping. Complications: rebleeding (first day), **vasospasm** with delayed ischaemia (days 3–14; oral **nimodipine** improves outcome), hydrocephalus, seizures and hyponatraemia (SIADH or cerebral salt wasting).',
'**Epidural haematoma**: a skull fracture at the pterion tears the middle meningeal artery; the patient may wake briefly (lucid interval), then deteriorates fast; the blood strips dura off bone but stops at the sutures, making a biconvex lens. **Subdural haematoma**: torn bridging veins between cortex and dural sinuses; elderly people with atrophy, alcohol, anticoagulants, shaken infants (with retinal haemorrhages); crescent-shaped, crosses sutures but not the midline falx; acute after major trauma, or chronic over weeks with fluctuating confusion.'],
table:{cols:['Bleed','Source','Clues'],rows:[
['Epidural','middle meningeal artery (pterion fracture)','lucid interval, then rapid decline; biconvex, stops at sutures'],
['Subdural','bridging veins','elderly, alcohol, anticoagulants, shaken infants; crescent, crosses sutures; acute or chronic'],
['Subarachnoid','berry aneurysm (anterior communicating commonest), AVM, trauma','thunderclap headache, neck stiffness; CT, then xanthochromia; vasospasm days 3–14 (nimodipine)'],
['Intraparenchymal','hypertension (putamen, thalamus, pons, cerebellum), amyloid angiopathy (lobar, elderly), AVM, tumour, anticoagulants','sudden focal deficit with headache, vomiting, falling consciousness']]},
key:'Uncal herniation: the medial temporal lobe slides under the tentorium, compressing CN III (blown pupil, then down and out), the ipsilateral PCA (occipital infarct) and the midbrain, whose opposite cerebral peduncle is pushed onto the tentorial edge (Kernohan notch: weakness on the same side as the mass, a false localising sign). Subfalcine herniation of the cingulate gyrus under the falx compresses the ACA (leg weakness). Downward central herniation tears brainstem perforators (Duret haemorrhages). Tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum crushes the medulla: apnoea, cardiovascular collapse, death.',
s:[['pharm','Reversing anticoagulants in an intracranial bleed: warfarin → four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate plus IV vitamin K; dabigatran → **idarucizumab** (a monoclonal antibody fragment that binds it); apixaban or rivaroxaban → **andexanet alfa** (a decoy factor Xa) or prothrombin complex concentrate; heparin → protamine. Lower systolic pressure toward 140 mmHg in intracerebral haemorrhage.']]},
{h:'After the blood stops',p:[
'Neurons die within minutes of losing flow, because the brain has almost no energy reserve. The core of an infarct is lost, but the surrounding **penumbra** is stunned rather than dead and can be rescued by fast reperfusion: time is brain. Brain infarcts undergo **liquefactive necrosis**, leaving a fluid-filled cavity. Embolic infarcts can bleed when the clot breaks up and blood returns to damaged vessels (haemorrhagic transformation). CT is often normal for the first hours and shows low density by 6–24 hours; diffusion-weighted MRI lights up within minutes.',
'**Watershed infarcts** follow global hypotension (cardiac arrest, shock) in the border zones between territories; between the ACA and MCA they cause proximal arm and leg weakness ("man in a barrel"). In global hypoxia the most vulnerable neurons die first: hippocampal CA1 cells, cerebellar Purkinje cells and the pyramidal neurons of neocortex layers 3, 5 and 6 (laminar necrosis).'],
table:{cols:['Time after infarct','What the microscope shows'],rows:[
['12–24 hours','red neurons: shrunken, intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm, pyknotic nuclei'],
['1–3 days','neutrophils arrive'],
['3–7 days','microglia and macrophages eat the debris (lipid-laden foamy cells)'],
['1–2 weeks','reactive astrocytes and new vessels at the edge'],
['weeks to months','a liquefied cavity walled off by a glial scar']]},
s:[['phys','**Raised intracranial pressure**: headache worse lying down and in the morning, vomiting, papilloedema, CN VI palsy, falling consciousness. The **Cushing reflex** is a late sign: hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing as the brainstem is compressed. Treatment: head up 30°, sedation, brief hyperventilation as a bridge (lower CO₂ constricts cerebral vessels), mannitol or hypertonic saline, CSF drainage or surgical decompression. Steroids reduce vasogenic oedema around tumours and abscesses but do not help after stroke or head injury.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Right arm and face weakness with halting, effortful speech and preserved comprehension. Artery and area?',o:['Right ACA; Broca area','Left PCA; Wernicke area','Basilar artery; pons','Left MCA superior division; Broca area'],a:3,d:'path',c:'stroke',x:'The superior division of the left (dominant) MCA supplies both Broca area and the face and arm motor strip, giving non-fluent aphasia with right face and arm weakness. Wernicke area belongs to the inferior division and causes fluent speech with poor comprehension.'},
{q:'Vertigo, hoarseness, difficulty swallowing, loss of pain and temperature on the left face and right body, and a left Horner syndrome. Artery?',o:['Left PICA','Right MCA','Basilar artery','Left ACA'],a:0,d:'path',c:'stroke',x:'This is the lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome: the nucleus ambiguus explains the hoarseness and dysphagia, and the face signs are on the side of the lesion. The basilar artery tempts because it is posterior circulation, but its strokes cause weakness and locked-in states rather than this sensory crossed pattern.'},
{q:'A brief lucid interval after a blow to the temple, then rapid decline into coma. CT appearance?',o:['Crescent that crosses sutures','Biconvex lens that stops at sutures','Blood filling the basal cisterns and sulci','A lobar parenchymal bleed'],a:1,d:'path',c:'stroke',x:'An epidural haematoma from the middle meningeal artery strips dura off the skull, and the dura\'s attachment at the sutures limits it to a lens shape. A crescent crossing the sutures is a subdural haematoma from bridging veins.'},
{q:'Five days after a subarachnoid haemorrhage she becomes drowsy with new arm weakness. Cause, and the drug given from day 1 to improve outcome?',o:['Rebleeding; aminocaproic acid','Hydrocephalus; mannitol','Vasospasm; nimodipine','Seizure; phenytoin'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'stroke',x:'Vasospasm with delayed cerebral ischaemia peaks between days 3 and 14, and oral nimodipine, a calcium channel blocker, improves outcome. Rebleeding is most likely in the first day and is prevented by securing the aneurysm early.'},
{q:'Contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing and no weakness. Artery?',o:['MCA','ACA','PCA','Anterior choroidal artery'],a:2,d:'path',c:'stroke',x:'The PCA supplies the occipital cortex, and the macular area is spared because it also receives MCA collaterals. An MCA stroke can cut the field too, but it brings face and arm weakness and cortical signs.'},
{q:'After a stroke a woman speaks fluently with good comprehension but cannot repeat "no ifs, ands or buts". Diagnosis?',o:['Broca aphasia','Conduction aphasia','Transcortical sensory aphasia','Wernicke aphasia'],a:1,d:'path',c:'stroke',x:'Fluent speech and intact comprehension with very poor repetition means the arcuate fasciculus linking Wernicke and Broca areas is cut. In transcortical sensory aphasia it is the opposite: comprehension is poor and repetition is preserved.'},
{q:'Sudden numbness of the whole left face, arm and leg, with no weakness, no field cut and normal language. Vessel?',o:['Thalamoperforating branches of the PCA','Lenticulostriate branches of the MCA','Anterior choroidal branch of the internal carotid','Superior division of the MCA'],a:0,d:'path',c:'hypertension',x:'A pure sensory lacune sits in the thalamic VPL, supplied by the thalamoperforating and thalamogeniculate branches of the posterior cerebral artery. The lenticulostriate arteries are the tempting answer, but they supply the internal capsule and cause pure motor strokes.'}
]};
